<v Ira Glass>每年冬天，在麻省理工学院都会举行一场活动，该国最优秀的解谜者会对抗最优秀的谜题作者。这叫麻省理工神秘大搜捕。它是一系列谜题——文字谜题、数字谜题、寻宝游戏、图片谜题，甚至没有名字的谜题。

00:32:30.690 --> 00:32:47.870
<v Ira Glass>And eventually what all this leads to-- this takes all weekend or longer-- what it leads to, the final answer, it leads you to a coin hidden somewhere on the MIT campus. This is a team competition. The teams are made up of MIT students, and also elite puzzle solvers who fly in from around the country for the event.
<v Ira Glass>最终所有这些通向——这需要整个周末或更长时间——最终答案会把你引向藏在麻省理工校园某处的硬币。这是一场团队竞赛。团队由麻省理工学生组成，还有从全国各地飞来参加活动的精英解谜者。

00:32:47.870 --> 00:33:08.120
<v Ira Glass>One of our producers, Lisa Pollak, joined them for last year's contest. She was following a team called Dr. Awkward. And for those of you who are playing along at home, Dr. Awkward is spelled, D-R period Awkward, A-W-K-W-A-R-D. Give yourself two points if you noticed that that is a palindrome, spelled the same forwards and backwards. Here's Lisa.
<v Ira Glass>我们的制作人之一，丽莎·波拉克，去年加入了他们。她跟随了一支叫“奥克沃德博士”的团队。对于那些在家玩的人，奥克沃德博士拼写为：D-R点奥克沃德，A-W-K-W-A-R-D。如果你注意到这是一个回文（正着念反着念都一样），给自己加两分。这是丽莎。

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:16.040
<v Lisa Pollak>When you hang out with a puzzle team, you hear a lot of stories like Dave Dickerson's. When Dave was just a kid, he got this book. He'll never forget the title.
<v Lisa Pollak>当你和谜题团队待在一起时，你会听到很多像戴夫·迪克森这样的故事。戴夫还是个孩子时，得到了这本书。他永远不会忘记那个标题。

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:17.940
<v Dave Dickerson>Puzzles , Puzzles , Puzzles, Puzzles, Puzzles.
<v Dave Dickerson>谜题，谜题，谜题，谜题，谜题。

00:33:17.940 --> 00:33:27.550
<v Lisa Pollak>Actually, the title might have just been Puzzles, but that's what the cover said. Dave was obsessed with this book. The puzzles were harder than any he'd ever seen, but he couldn't put them down.
<v Lisa Pollak>其实标题可能只是《谜题》，但封面就是那么写的。戴夫沉迷于这本书。谜题比他见过任何题目都难，但他停不下来。

00:33:27.550 --> 00:33:55.780
<v Dave Dickerson>And I even remember when I saw one of the clues-- not an elegant clue by modern standards-- but it said, damn, I'm trying to fix these socks. And the answer was darn. The darn socks. You know, needle and thread. And I thought, I get that. And I solved it. It was the first clue I had actually solved. And I thought, I can do this. I had never been challenged this much in my life. And I didn't realize I could do it.
<v Dave Dickerson>我甚至记得看到其中一个线索时——按照现代标准不算优雅——但它写着：该死，我正在尝试修理这些袜子。答案是“补”。补袜子。你知道，针和线。我想，我懂了。我就解决了。这是我第一个真正解开的线索。我想，我能做到。我一生中从没受到过如此大的挑战。我没意识到我能做到。

00:33:55.780 --> 00:34:12.520
<v Lisa Pollak>Not only could Dave do it, he found it impossible to stop doing it, which is how, almost 30 years later, he ended up here, standing in an MIT classroom with the rest of the Dr. Awkward team, warming up for the mystery hunt. And by warming up, I mean they were plugging in their laptops and setting up a snack table.
<v Lisa Pollak>戴夫不仅做到了，而且发现根本停不下来，这就是为什么将近30年后，他最终站在麻省理工学院的一间教室里，和“奥克沃德博士”团队的其他成员一起，为神秘大搜捕热身。所谓热身，就是他们插上笔记本电脑，摆好零食桌。

00:34:12.520 --> 00:34:30.880
<v Lisa Pollak>This was an experienced team with about 45 people on the roster. And as far as brainpower goes, they were an intimidating crew-- crossword champs, PhDs, a guy handing out DVDs of his recent appearance on Jeopardy, two guys whose T-shirts I needed help deciphering, and this man, whose nickname I'm still trying to figure out.
<v Lisa Pollak>这是一支经验丰富的团队，名单上有45人左右。就脑力而言，他们是一群令人生畏的家伙——填字游戏冠军、博士、一个正在分发他最近参加《危险边缘》DVD的人、两个我需要翻译T恤上文字的家伙，还有这个人，我还在试着找出他的绰号。

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:55.100
<v Kevin Wald>Ucaoimhu, spelled U-C-A-O-I-M-H-U. And it's from my real name, Kevin Wald. You split it up as KEV-IN W-ALD. Ald is an archaic form of the word "old," so you take an old-style "W", which is two "U's." You take "Kev" the way it's in the original Irish spelling, which is C-A-O-I-M-H. And you put it in the two "U's," so you get Ucaoimhu.
<v Kevin Wald>Ucaoimhu，拼写为U-C-A-O-I-M-H-U。它是取自我的真名，凯文·沃尔德。你把它拆开为KEV-IN W-ALD。Ald是单词“old”（老）的古语形式，所以你取一个古式的“W”，也就是两个“U”。你取爱尔兰原始拼写方式中的“Kev”，即C-A-O-I-M-H。把它放进两个“U”里，你就得到了Ucaoimhu。

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:58.740
<v Dave Dickerson>We're definitely a dangerous team. There's no question about it.
<v Dave Dickerson>我们绝对是一支危险的团队。毫无疑问。

00:34:58.740 --> 00:35:22.560
<v Lisa Pollak>That's Dave again. He's the person who invited me to hang out here. And he warned me that the puzzles I'd see his team solve that weekend wouldn't be what most of us imagine when we think of puzzles. Mystery hunt puzzles are so elaborate, so complex, that lots of times they don't even have instructions, just a page of words or pictures or numbers arranged in some cryptic way, which means you've often got to solve a puzzle just to know what puzzle you're trying to solve.
<v Lisa Pollak>又是戴夫。他是邀请我来这里的人。他警告我，那个周末我看到的谜题与我们大多数人想到谜题时所想象的不一样。神秘大搜捕的谜题如此精致、如此复杂，以至于很多时候它们甚至没有指令，只是一页以某种神秘方式排列的文字、图片或数字，这意味着你通常必须先解开一个谜题，才知道你正在试图解开什么谜题。

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:47.930
<v Dave Dickerson>So for example, the first puzzle hunt-- or maybe one of the first-- the guy who did it did a puzzle in Linear A, which is an old language. I think it's older than Greek, a very, very primitive, ancient language. And he didn't tell anyone it was Linear A. And he took the only two books on Linear A out of the MIT library. People solved it anyway.
<v Dave Dickerson>举个例子，第一个搜捕活动——或者最早的之一——做这个的人做了一个用线性文字A写的谜题，那是一种古老的语言。我想它比希腊语还古老，一种非常、非常原始的古代语言。他没告诉任何人那是线性文字A。而且他从麻省理工图书馆借走了仅有的两本关于线性文字A的书。人们还是把它解开了。

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:49.420
<v Man 1>Hey, everybody!
<v Man 1>嘿，大家！

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:51.910
<v Man 1>[CHEERS]
<v Man 1>[欢呼]

00:35:51.910 --> 00:35:52.860
<v Man 1>How you doing today?
<v Man 1>今天过得怎么样？

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:54.270
<v Man 1>[CHEERS]
<v Man 1>[欢呼]

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:55.210
<v Man 1>That's right.
<v Man 1>没错。

00:35:55.210 --> 00:36:22.280
<v Lisa Pollak>The mystery hunt started with an opening ceremony put on by last year's winning team. They're called The Evil Midnight Bombers What Bomb At Midnight. That's the way the hunt works. The group that won last year's hunt designs and runs this year's contest. Dozens of teams pack the lobby of an MIT building. And though they were competing, from what I could tell the mood seemed pretty friendly. The Evil Midnight Bombers kicked things off with a skit. Then all the teams headed off to various locations around campus, and the solving began.
<v Lisa Pollak>神秘大搜捕以去年的获胜团队举办的开幕式开始。他们叫“午夜爆炸的邪恶午夜轰炸机”。活动就是这样运作的。去年赢得搜捕活动的团体负责设计并运作今年的竞赛。几十支队伍挤在麻省理工的一栋大楼大厅里。尽管他们在竞争，但从我所见来看，气氛似乎相当友好。“邪恶午夜轰炸机”以一个小品开始了活动。然后所有队伍前往校园各处，解谜活动开始了。

00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:24.520
<v Lisa Pollak>[CHALK ON A CHALKBOARD]
<v Lisa Pollak>[粉笔在黑板上写的声响]

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:29.050
<v Jeremy>So what do you do with FNTLLLLLLSNN?
<v Jeremy>那么你用FNTLLLLLLSNN做什么？

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:33.470
<v Tripp>Those ?] just might be an index to something else. It might not even be an index to something in this round.
<v Tripp>那些[听不清]可能只是指向别的东西的索引。它甚至可能不是这一轮里某个东西的索引。

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:52.660
<v Lisa Pollak>That's Jeremy and [? Tripp, ?] two Dr. Awkward teammates, standing at a blackboard a few hours into the hunt. Now I'll be honest, as a spectator sport, competitive puzzle solving has some problems. Most of the time there wasn't much to see, just people huddled in small groups around laptops and conferring over printouts, batting around one idea after another until they hit on an answer.
<v Lisa Pollak>那是杰里米和[听不清]，两名奥克沃德博士队友，在搜捕开始几小时后站在黑板前。说实话，作为一项观赏性运动，竞技解谜有一些问题。大多数时候没啥可看的，只是一群人围着笔记本电脑挤在一起，对着打印稿商量，一个接一个地推敲想法，直到他们找到答案。

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:57.170
<v Woman 1>The third one is Gilligan's Island. Who's writing this down?
<v Woman 1>第三个是《吉利根岛》。谁记下来了？

00:36:57.170 --> 00:37:04.520
<v Lisa Pollak>Things seemed to be going pretty well. The blackboard was filling up with answers and new puzzles kept coming. But there were some stumpers, too.
<v Lisa Pollak>事情似乎进展得相当顺利。黑板上填满了答案，新的谜题不断出现。但也有一些让人卡住的题。

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:08.390
<v Man 2>And that doesn't look promising. So look at last letters.
<v Man 2>那看起来不太有希望。看看最后一个字母。

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:09.090
<v Man 3>SAX?
<v Man 3>SAX？

00:37:09.090 --> 00:37:36.140
<v Lisa Pollak>The name of this puzzle was Continental Divide. It was one of those no-instruction puzzles. There was a sheet of paper with a bunch of pictures of DVD movie covers on it. And the covers were arranged in a grid, six boxes across and five down. The bottom right-hand box was empty. All the titles on the covers were blacked out. Finding those was the easy part. It turns out all the films had a city, state, or country in the name. Raising Arizona , Road to Singapore , The Tailor of Panama . And so on.
<v Lisa Pollak>这个谜题的名字叫“大陆分界线”。它是那种没有指令的谜题之一。有一张纸，上面有一堆电影DVD封面的图片。封面排列成一个网格，横着六个格子，竖着五个。右下角的格子是空的。所有封面的标题都被抹黑了。找到那些标题是简单部分。结果所有电影名称里都有城市、州或国家。《挺进亚利桑那》、《通往新加坡之路》、《巴拿马裁缝》。等等。

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:51.130
<v Lisa Pollak>The answer, everyone agreed, had something to do with those place names, but what? Were they trying to put a title in the missing box? Spell a sentence from a combination of letters? I watched for more than an hour as at least a dozen different people tried together to figure it out.
<v Lisa Pollak>每个人都同意答案与那些地名有关，但到底是什么呢？他们是想在缺少的格子里填个标题吗？通过字母组合拼出一个句子吗？我看了超过一个小时，至少有十几个人试着一起弄清楚。

00:37:51.130 --> 00:37:57.780
<v Man 4>Annapolis, Dallas, Essex, Cancun. All of these in this column do not end in vowels.
<v Man 4>安纳波利斯、达拉斯、埃塞克斯、坎昆。这一列里的所有这些都不以元音结尾。

00:37:57.780 --> 00:38:06.740
<v Lisa Pollak>They plotted the names on a map. They typed them into a spreadsheet. They put them in a grid and they color coded them by continent. No pattern was too absurd to consider.
<v Lisa Pollak>他们把地名画在地图上。他们输入电子表格。他们把它们放在网格里并按大洲进行颜色编码。没有一种模式是荒谬到不考虑的。

00:38:06.740 --> 00:38:10.920
<v Eric>Arizona Iced Tea, Singapore sling, Panama hat. I still think we might be able to get drinks out of them.
<v Eric>亚利桑那冰茶、新加坡司令、巴拿马帽。我还是觉得我们可以从它们那里得到饮料名。

00:38:10.920 --> 00:38:12.140
<v Man 5>India pale ale.
<v Man 5>印度淡色艾尔啤酒。

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:14.940
<v Eric>India pale ale and-- OK, yeah. These are drinks across.
<v Eric>印度淡色艾尔啤酒和——好的，没错。这些是横向的饮料名。

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:16.710
<v Man 5>Oh, my God.
<v Man 5>噢，天哪。

00:38:16.710 --> 00:38:18.070
<v Lisa Pollak>They could not have been more wrong.
<v Lisa Pollak>他们不可能错得更离谱了。

00:38:18.070 --> 00:38:24.160
<v Eric>I like the idea from half an hour ago where we were mapping these things to letters.
<v Eric>我喜欢半小时前关于把这些东西映射到字母上的想法。

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:43.440
<v Lisa Pollak>The answer ended up having something to do with DVD country codes. But they didn't know that yet. For now, despite all the work, they weren't getting anywhere. At times I couldn't believe they were actually doing this for fun. But for puzzle people, the struggle is part of the fun. It's not in vain, because they know that somewhere out there there's an answer.
<v Lisa Pollak>答案最后与DVD国家码有关。但他们还不知道。目前，尽管付出了所有努力，他们还是毫无进展。有时我简直不敢相信他们真的把这当成乐趣。但对于玩谜题的人来说，奋斗也是乐趣的一部分。这并非徒劳，因为他们知道在某个地方存在着答案。

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:49.420
<v Eric>I mean, somebody can say something in the next 15 seconds that will just break this whole thing wide open.
<v Eric>我是说，某个人在接下来的15秒内说出的话，就能让这一切豁然开朗。

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:59.870
<v Lisa Pollak>That's Eric, a veteran of the team. And what he's talking about there, that flash of insight, that aha moment when something suddenly becomes clear, that's the payoff.
<v Lisa Pollak>那是埃里克，团队老手。他所谈论的那一点灵感，那个突然变得清晰的“啊哈”瞬间，就是回报。

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:11.550
<v Eric>An observation that nobody else saw, seeing through the problem. And that's difficult to do. And I'm not going to do it on every puzzle. I'm not one of the geniuses around here. But I am able to contribute something, and I'm just waiting for that to happen.
<v Eric>别人没看到的观察，看穿问题。这很难做到。我不会在每个谜题上都做到这一点。我不是这儿的天才。但我能贡献一些东西，我正在等待那一刻发生。

00:39:11.550 --> 00:39:24.470
<v Lisa Pollak>You don't have to solve a puzzle to have an aha moment. The puzzles, if you're a puzzle person, are a pretty reliable way to get them, as opposed to real life, where, as a team member named John pointed out to me, most problems don't come with their own answers.
<v Lisa Pollak>你不必解开谜题才能有“啊哈”瞬间。如果你是玩谜题的人，谜题是获得那种瞬间相当可靠的方式，这与现实生活不同，正如团队成员约翰向我指出的那样，现实生活中的大多数问题并没有自带答案。

00:39:24.470 --> 00:39:50.270
<v John>You know, it's very different trying to figure out, why does my daughter hate me? And how can I help her? Because there's no solution to that. Versus, OK, what are those numbers in the background of the evil video? Are they trying to say some message there? This you get the joy of, OK. At least you got the answer, and you know that you got the answer.
<v John>你知道，试图弄清楚“为什么我女儿讨厌我？以及我该如何帮助她？”是非常不同的。因为那个没有解决方案。对比，“好的，那个邪恶视频背景里的数字是什么？他们是在试图表达某种信息吗？”这个你能享受到，“好的，至少你得到了答案，你知道你得到了答案。”

00:39:50.270 --> 00:39:53.150
<v John>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v John>[交谈声]

00:39:53.150 --> 00:40:02.500
<v Kevin Wald>It is right now 1:23 in the morning. And we're in the sixth round. We've got tons and tons of puzzles in these rounds.
<v Kevin Wald>现在是凌晨1:23。我们在第六轮。这些轮次里有成吨的谜题。

00:40:02.500 --> 00:40:23.930
<v Lisa Pollak>When I caught up with Ucaoimhu, the Dr. Awkward team had been solving puzzles for 13 and 1/2 straight hours. The desks were covered with empty food containers. And the classroom was starting to smell like a dorm room whose residents hadn't showered in a while. Some team members had gone home to sleep. And a night crew with fresher brains had shown up to relieve them. But Ucaoimhu, like a lot of the 20 or so people here, had been there since the start.
<v Lisa Pollak>当我追上Ucaoimhu时，奥克沃德博士团队已经连续解谜13个半小时了。书桌上覆盖着空的食品盒。教室开始闻起来像那种住户很久没洗澡的宿舍。一些团队成员回家睡觉了，换了一批头脑更清醒的夜班人员来接替他们。但像这里大约20人中的许多人一样，Ucaoimhu从一开始就在那儿了。

00:40:23.930 --> 00:40:25.650
<v Lisa Pollak>How are you feeling right now?
<v Lisa Pollak>你现在感觉如何？

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:35.530
<v Kevin Wald>Right now, I'm feeling tired. And I'll probably leave within the next hour or so. Fortunately, what I'm working on now is a nice, gentle kind of puzzle.
<v Kevin Wald>现在，我觉得累了。我可能会在接下来一小时左右离开。幸运的是，我现在处理的是一种既优雅又温和的谜题。

00:40:35.530 --> 00:40:57.090
<v Lisa Pollak>And by a nice, gentle kind of puzzle, what Ucaoimhu meant was a grid that looked kind of like a crossword puzzle, only with no blacked-in squares, no numbers, and no clues. I repeat, no clues at all, just empty boxes. Oh, and in the background, a red cross, like on an English flag. And yes, they solved it.
<v Lisa Pollak>所谓既优雅又温和的谜题，Ucaoimhu指的是一个看起来像填字游戏的网格，只是没有填黑的方块，没有数字，也没有线索。我再说一遍，根本没有线索，只有空的方块。噢，背景里有个红十字，就像英国国旗上的一样。是的，他们解开了。

00:40:57.090 --> 00:41:17.340
<v Lisa Pollak>I stuck around that night watching until 6:00 in the morning. And what struck me about the whole scene was how matter of fact everyone was, like there was nothing out of the ordinary here, as if this was the most normal thing in the world, men and women, some college students, but plenty with spouses and kids at home, staying up all night in a college classroom solving puzzles.
<v Lisa Pollak>那天晚上我一直待到凌晨6点。整个场景让我印象深刻的是每个人表现得多么理所当然，好像这里没发生什么不寻常的事，好像这是世界上最正常的事情：男人和女人，有些是大学生，但有很多在家有配偶和孩子，整晚待在大学教室里解谜。

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:42.830
<v Lisa Pollak>But the next day, as the hunt stretched into its 30th hour, I caught up with Dave, the guy who invited me here. And he told me that, as a puzzle person, he was constantly being reminded that the rest of the world doesn't work this way. Just the other day, he'd gone into a bar. And when the female bartender came over, he told her about this cool new anagram he'd heard, how if you take the phrase, a dream within a dream, and rearrange the letters, you get, what am I? A mind reader.
<v Lisa Pollak>但在第二天，当搜捕活动延长到第30小时，我追上了戴夫，那个邀请我来这里的人。他告诉我，作为一名玩谜题的人，他经常被提醒，世界其他地方不是这样运作的。就在前几天，他进了一家酒吧。当女酒保走过来时，他告诉她他听到的一个超酷的新变位词，说如果你取“a dream within a dream”（梦中之梦）这个短语，重新排列字母，你就能得到“what am I? A mind reader”（我是什么？读心者）。

00:41:42.830 --> 00:41:58.750
<v Lisa Pollak>This didn't go over so well. The bartender-- apparently finding this fact neither interesting nor charming-- just looked at Dave like he was weird. Dave told me that years ago, while he was working as a greeting card writer for Hallmark in Kansas City, he actually got in trouble for doing this kind of thing.
<v Lisa Pollak>这没那么好用。那个酒保——显然觉得这个事实既没趣也不迷人——只是看着戴夫，觉得他很奇怪。戴夫告诉我，几年前，当他在堪萨斯城的霍尔马克做贺卡撰稿人时，他确实因为做这种事惹上了麻烦。

00:41:58.750 --> 00:42:13.090
<v Dave Dickerson>My mentor came to me and said, Dave, we have to talk. And he said, Dave, you're using too many literary allusions in your casual speech and people are complaining.
<v Dave Dickerson>我的导师来找我说，戴夫，我们得谈谈。他说，戴夫，你在日常谈话中用了太多的文学典故，人们在抱怨。

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:16.420
<v Lisa Pollak>Wait, wait. In your speech, not in your greeting cards?
<v Lisa Pollak>等等，等等。是在你的谈话中，而不是在贺卡里？

00:42:16.420 --> 00:42:31.470
<v Dave Dickerson>Right, right, just in my everyday speech. I knew better than to use obscure allusions in greeting cards, goodness gracious. I asked my supervisor, what the hell does that even mean? And he said, I was just told to tell it to you.
<v Dave Dickerson>对，对，只是在我的日常谈话中。我知道不能在贺卡里用生僻的典故，老天保佑。我问主管，那到底是什么意思？他说，我只是被要求来告诉你的。

00:42:31.470 --> 00:42:42.500
<v Lisa Pollak>Dave started obsessing about this. He wasn't even sure what the problem was, so he began keeping track. One week, every time he had the urge to say something at work, he wrote it in a notebook instead.
<v Lisa Pollak>戴夫开始纠结这件事。他甚至不确定问题出在哪儿，所以他开始做记录。有一周，每次他在工作中冲动想说点什么时，他就把它写在笔记本里。

00:42:42.500 --> 00:42:49.290
<v Dave Dickerson>By the end of that week, my notebook was full of the most random crap. None of it was a literary allusion.
<v Dave Dickerson>到那周末，我的笔记本里记满了最随机的垃圾。没一个是文学典故。

00:42:49.290 --> 00:43:00.070
<v Lisa Pollak>Maybe, Dave thought, the problem wasn't him. He'd recently been transferred from the humor department to the serious writing staff. And he was surrounded by all these new people who probably just didn't understand him.
<v Lisa Pollak>也许，戴夫想，问题不在他身上。他最近从幽默部门转到了严肃写作组。他身边全是这些可能根本不理解他的新人。

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:25.930
<v Dave Dickerson>I was still doing lunch with the guys from humor. That was what I felt was my real home, and I could be myself there. And so I was at lunch with them maybe two or three weeks later, and one of the guys, as we were going to lunch, said, you know-- and it was a cluster of us, five or six of us, all heading toward the lunch room. And one of the guys said, you know, we do a lot of monkey cards. And yet--
<v Dave Dickerson>我还是和幽默组的伙计们一起吃午饭。我觉得那是我的真家，我能在那里做回自己。所以两三周后我和他们一起吃午饭，其中一个伙计，当我们去吃午饭时，说，你知道——那是我们一群人，五六个，都往食堂走。其中一个伙计说，你知道，我们做了很多猴子贺卡。然而——

00:43:25.930 --> 00:43:27.930
<v Lisa Pollak>You mean cards with pictures of monkeys?
<v Lisa Pollak>你是说印有猴子图片的贺卡？

00:43:27.930 --> 00:44:07.010
<v Dave Dickerson>Yes, yes, more or less, because what he actually said was, and yet, they're all illustrated with chimpanzees. And chimpanzees aren't monkeys, are they? And I said-- because I had done a report on this in fourth grade, and because it was something of an obsession of mine, monkeys, apes, and so on-- was I said, actually, chimpanzees are apes, along with orangutans, gorillas, and gibbons. And in fact, one of the weird things about Planet of the Apes is they don't have any gibbons in them. The orangutans that they claim are orangutans look like gibbons. And the way you distinguish apes from monkeys is apes have no tails and they're not exclusively arboreal.
<v Dave Dickerson>是的，是的，差不多，因为他实际说的是，然而它们都是用黑猩猩来做插图。黑猩猩不是猴子，对吧？我说——因为我在四年级时做过关于这个的报告，而且因为这是我的一种痴迷，猴子、类人猿等等——我说，其实黑猩猩是类人猿，还有红毛猩猩、大猩猩和长臂猿。事实上，关于《人猿星球》的一个怪事是他们里面没有长臂猿。他们声称是红毛猩猩的看起来像长臂猿。区分猿和猴子的方法是猿没有尾巴，而且不是完全树栖的。

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:29.580
<v Dave Dickerson>And there was this pause. And I said, actually, there's a whole subset of other animals that are like monkeys called prosimians. And they include the lemur, and the tarsier, and the kinkajou, and the galago-- also called the bush baby-- and they're really goofy looking. And boy, if they had a Planet of the Prosimians, I would totally watch that movie.
<v Dave Dickerson>停顿了一下。我说，其实还有一整类其他动物像猴子，叫原猴亚目。它们包括狐猴、眼镜猴、蜜熊和婴猴——也叫丛林婴儿——它们看起来真的很滑稽。天哪，如果他们有一部《原猴星球》，我绝对会看那部电影。

00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:55.950
<v Dave Dickerson>And there was a further pause. And one of my friends said, oh, Dave, hey, speaking of animals, would you like to see the rat's ass that I give? And I thought, oh, that's my problem. I inform people against their will.
<v Dave Dickerson>又停顿了一下。我的一个朋友说，噢，戴夫，嘿，说到动物，你想看我哪怕一点点在乎吗？我想，噢，那是我的问题。我强行告知人们他们不想知道的信息。

00:44:55.950 --> 00:45:03.970
<v Lisa Pollak>So what you're saying is that the thing that can be very annoying in the real world is the same thing that makes you really good at solving puzzles?
<v Lisa Pollak>所以你的意思是，在现实世界中非常恼人的东西，同时也是让你在解谜方面非常出色的东西？

00:45:03.970 --> 00:45:39.710
<v Dave Dickerson>Yes, yes. Although I also feel obliged to point out that perhaps the more important point-- as far as I'm concerned-- is the thing that makes me annoying in the regular world is not annoying here. This environment is one of the only places where I can say something like that and not worry that I might be irritating someone by saying it, that even if it's not relevant, people at least understand the impulse.
<v Dave Dickerson>是的，是的。尽管我也觉得有必要指出，也许更重要的一点——就我而言——是那个在现实世界中让我恼人的东西在这里并不恼人。这个环境是唯一能让我说出那种话，而不担心可能会惹恼别人的地方，即使它不相关，人们至少能理解那种冲动。

00:45:39.710 --> 00:45:58.800
<v Lisa Pollak>On a puzzle team, Dave can be himself only better. And I think this is true for a lot of people whose talents require the right context in which to shine. Think about it. A boxer without a boxing ring is just a guy punching people. In a puzzle competition, a guy with a mind for obscure facts can be a star.
<v Lisa Pollak>在谜题团队里，戴夫可以做回他自己，而且更好。我认为这对于许多才华需要合适的背景才能闪耀的人来说也是如此。想想看。没有拳击台的拳击手只是个打人的人。在谜题竞赛中，一个拥有大量生僻知识储备的家伙就能成为明星。

00:45:58.800 --> 00:45:59.300
<v Dave Dickerson>Yes.
<v Dave Dickerson>是的。

00:45:59.300 --> 00:46:03.760
<v Dave Dickerson>[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]
<v Dave Dickerson>[欢呼和掌声]

00:46:03.760 --> 00:46:04.750
<v Man 6>Good job, guys.
<v Man 6>干得好，伙计们。

00:46:04.750 --> 00:46:08.670
<v Man 7>Good work.
<v Man 7>干得漂亮。

00:46:08.670 --> 00:46:34.610
<v Ira Glass>Lisa Pollak. The 2007 MIT Mystery Hunt was won in 38 hours and 14 minutes by none other than the Dr. Awkward team, whose members immediately began planning for this year's hunt, which was won two weeks ago by the team, Evil Midnight Bombers What Bomb at Midnight. If you'd like to try some of these mystery hunt puzzles yourself, you can find them all by Googling MIT Mystery Hunt. And God have mercy on your soul.
<v Ira Glass>丽莎·波拉克。2007年麻省理工神秘大搜捕由“奥克沃德博士”团队以38小时14分钟夺冠，其成员立即开始规划今年的搜捕活动，该活动两周前被“午夜爆炸的邪恶午夜轰炸机”团队赢走。如果你想亲自尝试一些这些神秘搜捕谜题，可以通过谷歌搜索“MIT Mystery Hunt”找到它们。愿上帝怜悯你的灵魂。

00:46:34.610 --> 00:46:51.280
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SMARTER THAN YOU" BY THE UNDERTONES]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 送葬者乐队的《Smarter Than You》]

00:46:51.280 --> 00:46:54.760
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Girls in Need of a Safer Time.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，需要更安全时代的女孩们。

00:46:54.760 --> 00:47:17.680
<v Ira Glass>Robin Epstein remembers the ad. There was a hospital nursery full of babies. And one baby girl tosses off her pink knit cap. It lands on the floor. This inspires other little baby girls to do the same. Pink hats come flying off of heads. Finally, the first little baby girl raises her little baby fist into the air, as Helen Reddy sings, "I Am Woman."
<v Ira Glass>罗宾·爱泼斯坦记得那个广告。医院的婴儿室里满是婴儿。一个小女婴甩掉了她的粉色针织帽。它掉在地上。这激励了其他小女婴也这样做。粉色的帽子从头上飞落。最后，第一个小女婴把她的小拳头举向空中，海伦·瑞迪唱着《我是女人》。

00:47:17.680 --> 00:47:23.640
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "I AM WOMAN" BY HELEN REDDY]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 海伦·瑞迪的《I Am Woman》]

00:47:23.640 --> 00:47:35.740
<v Ira Glass>Please, don't stand up in your cars. This was an ad for the Oxygen Network, all-women's network, just about to go on the air, February 2, 2000, which Robin found completely thrilling.
<v Ira Glass>求求你们，别在车里站起来。这是氧气电视网的广告，一个专门针对女性的网络，即将于2000年2月2日开播，罗宾觉得这极其激动人心。

00:47:35.740 --> 00:47:47.150
<v Robin Epstein>It was this really wonderful moment of, here we go. It sort of heralded our arrival, and the birth of the network, and the excitement behind it. And it felt like it was something really to cheer for.
<v Robin Epstein>那是极其美妙的一刻，我们开始了。它某种程度上预示了我们的到来，以及网络的诞生，以及背后的兴奋感。感觉它确实是值得欢呼的事情。

00:47:47.150 --> 00:47:59.300
<v Ira Glass>Robin had just gotten a job on a brand-new show on this brand-new network. It was a quiz show for teenage girls called Clued In. Robin was going to write all the questions in the quiz. And she did this with a real sense of mission.
<v Ira Glass>罗宾刚在这个全新网络的全新节目中找到了一份工作。这是一个针对少女的智力竞赛节目，叫《Clued In》。罗宾要为节目编写所有问题。她带着真正的使命感完成了这项工作。

00:47:59.300 --> 00:48:10.760
<v Robin Epstein>It just seemed like a fantastic idea for a show to me, which was to get teenage girls on a quiz show and to show the world, really, how smart they were.
<v Robin Epstein>对我来说，这似乎是一个精彩的节目构想，那就是让少女参加智力竞赛节目，真正向世界展示她们有多聪明。

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:13.220
<v Ira Glass>Show the world and show other teenage girls?
<v Ira Glass>展示给世界和向其他少女展示？

00:48:13.220 --> 00:48:20.740
<v Robin Epstein>Absolutely. And I wanted to show that these were the girls that you should be looking up to, that there were, in fact, role models that you weren't seeing.
<v Robin Epstein>绝对。而且我想展示她们应该是你仰慕的女孩，事实上确实有你没见过的榜样。

00:48:20.740 --> 00:48:23.050
<v Ira Glass>Right, and they walk among us. They're everyday girls.
<v Ira Glass>对，她们就在我们中间。她们是普通女孩。

00:48:23.050 --> 00:48:23.700
<v Robin Epstein>That's right.
<v Robin Epstein>没错。

00:48:23.700 --> 00:48:29.220
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Did you know about these studies at the time that-- I think the researcher was named Gilligan.
<v Ira Glass>对。你当时知道那些研究吗——我想研究者叫吉利根。

00:48:29.220 --> 00:49:04.990
<v Robin Epstein>Yeah, Carol Gilligan. Absolutely. Yes. I think it was basically until the age of 11 or so, girls in class, they are constantly raising their hands. It's important that they look smart. They want to impress their teachers. And so they're constantly all about giving the right answer, and reading, and researching, and whatnot. And then there's something that happens to these girls at age 12 or 13 where it inverts. And suddenly, appearing smart is not important at all for the majority of them.
<v Robin Epstein>对，卡罗尔·吉利根。绝对是的。我想研究基本上说，直到11岁左右，课堂上的女孩总是举手。她们看起来很聪明很重要。她们想给老师留下印象。所以她们总是关于给出正确答案、阅读、研究之类。然后在12或13岁时，这些女孩身上发生了一些变化，它倒置了。突然间，看起来聪明对于她们中的大多数人来说一点也不重要了。

00:49:04.990 --> 00:49:08.790
<v Ira Glass>And when you began this show, did you know about that research? And did you believe it?
<v Ira Glass>当你开始这个节目时，你知道那个研究吗？你相信它吗？

00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:36.350
<v Robin Epstein>I absolutely knew about the research. I didn't buy it. I thought, no way. I mean, sort of based on my own experiences in school, I was in a public school, but a lot of my friends were really smart girls. And we were all sort of very pleased with being high achievers and doing all this stuff. And I just thought, that research is a crock. You know, where is this woman going? What schools? How many girls did she actually interview?
<v Robin Epstein>我绝对知道那个研究。我不买账。我想，没门。我的意思是，基于我自己上学的经历，我是在公立学校，但我有很多朋友都是非常聪明的女孩。我们都因为成为高成就者并做所有这些事而感到非常高兴。我只是想，那个研究是胡扯。你知道，这个女人要去哪儿？什么学校？她到底采访了多少女孩？

00:49:36.350 --> 00:49:36.850
<v Robin Epstein>[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]
<v Robin Epstein>[欢呼和掌声]

00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:41.340
<v Ian Kesler>Welcome back. Welcome back. I'm Ian Kesler, and it's time to get Clued In.
<v Ian Kesler>欢迎回来。欢迎回来。我是伊恩·凯斯勒，是时候开始《Clued In》了。

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:47.840
<v Ira Glass>And just describe what the early shows were like, what kinds of questions there were, how people would respond.
<v Ira Glass>描述一下早期的节目是什么样的，有什么样的问题，人们会如何反应。

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:59.420
<v Robin Epstein>Right. So in the early shows, we would ask a range of questions. And I thought that there was a baseline of things that clued-in people should be aware of and should know.
<v Robin Epstein>对。所以早期节目中，我们会问一系列问题。我以为有些东西是聪明人应该了解和知道的基准。

00:49:59.420 --> 00:50:07.410
<v Ian Kesler>All right, here's the question. When stocks rise, it's a bull market. What animal symbolizes a market decline?
<v Ian Kesler>好，这是问题。当股票上涨时，它是牛市。什么动物象征市场下跌？

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:08.290
<v Ian Kesler>[BUZZER]
<v Ian Kesler>[蜂鸣器]

00:50:08.290 --> 00:50:08.870
<v Ian Kesler>Allison.
<v Ian Kesler>艾莉森。

00:50:08.870 --> 00:50:10.040
<v Allison>Chicken?
<v Allison>鸡？

00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:12.760
<v Ian Kesler>No. Good guess, but that wasn't it. Janine.
<v Ian Kesler>不。猜得不错，但不是那个。珍妮。

00:50:12.760 --> 00:50:13.500
<v Janine>A bird?
<v Janine>鸟？

00:50:13.500 --> 00:50:16.800
<v Ian Kesler>No, that's incorrect. Do you want to give it a shot, Jacqueline?
<v Ian Kesler>不，那个不对。想试试吗，杰奎琳？

00:50:16.800 --> 00:50:17.280
<v Jacqueline>No.
<v Jacqueline>不。

00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:22.910
<v Ian Kesler>No, you don't? You don't want to embarrass yourself? All right, so actually it's a bear. It's a bear market. OK, don't worry about it.
<v Ian Kesler>不，你不？你不想让自己尴尬？好吧，其实是熊。熊市。好的，别担心。

00:50:22.910 --> 00:50:24.200
<v Ira Glass>So is that pretty typical?
<v Ira Glass>这是典型情况吗？

00:50:24.200 --> 00:50:40.910
<v Robin Epstein>Yes. There started to be a creeping sensation that the questions were way, way too hard when she came out with chicken. That's where I thought, oh, OK, we have a problem.
<v Robin Epstein>是的。当她说出鸡的时候，开始有一种令人不安的感觉，问题太难了。那就是我认为，噢，好的，我们有问题。

00:50:40.910 --> 00:50:50.810
<v Ian Kesler>Here we go. 500-point follow-up, same category. What losing presidential candidate lost the use of his hand in World War II? Three seconds. Emily.
<v Ian Kesler>开始。500分跟进题，同一类别。哪位竞选总统失败的候选人在二战中失去了手的使用权？三秒。艾米丽。

00:50:50.810 --> 00:50:52.220
<v Emily>Teddy Roosevelt?
<v Emily>泰迪·罗斯福？

00:50:52.220 --> 00:51:00.430
<v Ian Kesler>No. Anybody else want to give it a shot? Come on. Bob Dole. Bob Dole. Yeah, World War II.
<v Ian Kesler>不。还有人想试试吗？来吧。鲍勃·多尔。鲍勃·多尔。是的，二战。

00:51:00.430 --> 00:51:09.270
<v Ira Glass>With the girls out there not able to get answers, not able to get these answers, did you ever feel a little guilty? You know what I mean? Like you're doing them a disservice, you're making them look bad.
<v Ira Glass>看着女孩们答不出问题，答不出这些问题，你有没有感到一点内疚？你知道我的意思吗？就像你帮了倒忙，你让她们看起来很糟糕。

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:43.450
<v Robin Epstein>Oh, absolutely, every day. It was one of these things where, when they had the blank stares and they were not ringing in on their buzzers, I would sort of sit there and I just had my hand on my forehead, sort of looking down, and just feeling like, what have I done? Like I have put them in this position. It is my fault that they can not answer, that in fact, not only am I not showing that girls are smart, I have put on the air that girls are stupid.
<v Robin Epstein>噢，绝对，每天。这是那种情况之一，当她们一脸空白，没有按下蜂鸣器时，我会坐在那儿，把手放在额头上，向下看着，感觉自己做了什么？就像我把她们置于这种位置。她们回答不上来是我的错，事实上，我不但没展示女孩们聪明，反而播出了女孩们很笨的节目。

00:51:43.450 --> 00:51:49.310
<v Ian Kesler>That's really good. And actually, Lauren, that's wrong, too. So you don't get any points. I'm sorry. All right. Jasmine, are you ready? Here's your first question.
<v Ian Kesler>这非常好。而且事实上，劳伦，那个也错了。所以你没分。很抱歉。好的。茉莉，准备好了吗？这是你的第一个问题。

00:51:49.310 --> 00:51:52.250
<v Ira Glass>Now, this is a show on a network for women--
<v Ira Glass>现在，这是一个女性网络上的节目——

00:51:52.250 --> 00:51:52.540
<v Robin Epstein>Yes.
<v Robin Epstein>是的。

00:51:52.540 --> 00:51:54.210
<v Ira Glass>--and it's aimed at girls.
<v Ira Glass>——而且是针对女孩的。

00:51:54.210 --> 00:51:54.930
<v Robin Epstein>Yes.
<v Robin Epstein>是的。

00:51:54.930 --> 00:51:57.400
<v Ira Glass>Why is the host a guy?
<v Ira Glass>为什么主持人是个男的？

00:51:57.400 --> 00:52:10.770
<v Robin Epstein>Yeah, that is a great question. We actually auditioned scores of women to be hosts of the show. There was one girl who was possibly a good fit for the show.
<v Robin Epstein>是啊，这是个好问题。我们确实试镜了许多女性来做主持人。有一个女孩可能是节目的好人选。

00:52:10.770 --> 00:52:12.100
<v Ira Glass>And?
<v Ira Glass>然后呢？

00:52:12.100 --> 00:52:14.930
<v Robin Epstein>And she was not really pretty enough to be on TV.
<v Robin Epstein>她长得不够漂亮，不能上电视。

00:52:14.930 --> 00:52:15.400
<v Ira Glass>Are you serious?
<v Ira Glass>你是认真的吗？

00:52:15.400 --> 00:52:15.800
<v Robin Epstein>Mmm-hmm.
<v Robin Epstein>嗯哼。

00:52:15.800 --> 00:52:18.440
<v Ira Glass>She was good, but she wasn't just pretty enough?
<v Ira Glass>她很好，但就是不够漂亮？

00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:19.840
<v Robin Epstein>Yes.
<v Robin Epstein>是的。

00:52:19.840 --> 00:52:22.300
<v Ira Glass>Even on this sort of pro-woman network?
<v Ira Glass>即便是在这种所谓的支持女性的网络上？

00:52:22.300 --> 00:52:25.960
<v Robin Epstein>Even on the we-love-you-for-what's-on-the-inside network. Yes.
<v Robin Epstein>即便是在那种“我们因为你的内在而爱你”的网络上。是的。

00:52:25.960 --> 00:52:31.550
<v Ira Glass>And what did you think when you saw that go down? Did you just feel like, OK, well, whatever, it's television?
<v Ira Glass>当你看到这种情况发生时，你是怎么想的？你只是觉得，噢，好吧，随便啦，反正那是电视？

00:52:31.550 --> 00:52:48.440
<v Robin Epstein>Yeah. I mean, we were TV people, and so there-- and the assumption also was that if we wanted teen girls to be interested, maybe we give them a little eye candy. And the host that we chose sort of had a passing resemblance to Matt Damon. He was like a Matt Damon type.
<v Robin Epstein>是的。我的意思是，我们是电视人，所以那里——而且假设也是，如果我们想让少女感兴趣，也许我们需要提供一点眼球福利。我们选择的主持人长得有点像马特·达蒙。他属于马特·达蒙类型。

00:52:48.440 --> 00:52:52.180
<v Ian Kesler>And here's the question. What amendment gave women the right to vote?
<v Ian Kesler>这是问题。哪项修正案赋予女性投票权？

00:52:52.180 --> 00:52:53.070
<v Ian Kesler>[BUZZER]
<v Ian Kesler>[蜂鸣器]

00:52:53.070 --> 00:52:53.350
<v Ian Kesler>[? Kocey. ?]
<v Ian Kesler>[听不清]科西。

00:52:53.350 --> 00:52:55.210
<v Woman 2>There you go, [? Kocey. ?] It's all you.
<v Woman 2>给你，科西。全看你了。

00:52:55.210 --> 00:52:58.840
<v Woman 3>Eighth? Ninth? 14th? 15th? Eighth?
<v Woman 3>第八？第九？第14？第15？第八？

00:52:58.840 --> 00:52:59.390
<v Ian Kesler>Three seconds.
<v Ian Kesler>三秒。

00:52:59.390 --> 00:53:01.970
<v Woman 3>Ninth? Eighth? Ninth? Say Ninth.
<v Woman 3>第九？第八？第九？说第九。

00:53:01.970 --> 00:53:03.890
<v Woman 4>The Ninth. No, wait.
<v Woman 4>第九。不，等等。

00:53:03.890 --> 00:53:05.340
<v Woman 3>No, no, she didn't say it.
<v Woman 3>不，不，她没说。

00:53:05.340 --> 00:53:05.830
<v Ian Kesler>She said Ninth.
<v Ian Kesler>她说了第九。

00:53:05.830 --> 00:53:07.010
<v Ian Kesler>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Ian Kesler>[交谈声]

00:53:07.010 --> 00:53:08.380
<v Woman 5>I'm going to guess 14th.
<v Woman 5>我打算猜第14。

00:53:08.380 --> 00:53:12.100
<v Ian Kesler>That's incorrect. Actually, it's the 19th Amendment. 19th Amendment.
<v Ian Kesler>那个不对。事实上是第19修正案。第19修正案。

00:53:12.100 --> 00:53:14.450
<v 6>None of us deserve the right to vote, because we don't know that.
<v 6>我们中没人应得投票权，因为我们不知道那个。

00:53:14.450 --> 00:53:16.580
<v Ian Kesler>That's true, exactly. So nobody gets the final question.
<v Ian Kesler>那是真的，没错。所以没人答对最后一题。

00:53:16.580 --> 00:53:19.430
<v Ira Glass>None of us deserves the right to vote. Yes, exactly.
<v Ira Glass>我们中没人应得投票权。是的，没错。

00:53:19.430 --> 00:53:21.210
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:53:21.210 --> 00:53:26.560
<v Ira Glass>So the girls can't answer most of the questions that you designed for the show. What do you do?
<v Ira Glass>所以女孩们回答不上你为节目设计的大多数问题。你怎么办？

00:53:26.560 --> 00:53:38.260
<v Robin Epstein>You dumb down questions. You give them things that anyone of any age, of any mental capacity could possibly answer.
<v Robin Epstein>你把问题变简单。你给她们任何年龄、任何智力水平的人都能回答的东西。

00:53:38.260 --> 00:53:40.260
<v Ian Kesler>Jesus is known as the son of whom?
<v Ian Kesler>耶稣被称为谁的儿子？

00:53:40.260 --> 00:53:41.520
<v Ian Kesler>[BUZZER]
<v Ian Kesler>[蜂鸣器]

00:53:41.520 --> 00:53:41.950
<v Ian Kesler>[? Fane. ?]
<v Ian Kesler>[听不清]费恩。

00:53:41.950 --> 00:53:42.630
<v Woman 6>God.
<v Woman 6>上帝。

00:53:42.630 --> 00:53:47.940
<v Ian Kesler>God, that's right. So you're up to 4,000 points. Abby, you've got 3,250. Which one do you want to go with?
<v Ian Kesler>上帝，没错。所以你有4000分了。艾比，你有3250分。你想选哪一个？

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:48.790
<v Abby>All right, I'll take This Spells Disaster.
<v Abby>好的，我选“拼写灾难”。

00:53:48.790 --> 00:53:54.580
<v Ian Kesler>This Spells Disaster. Here's the question. Without fumbling, who can spell her first name backwards?
<v Ian Kesler>拼写灾难。这是问题。不许结巴，谁能把她的名字倒着拼出来？

00:53:54.580 --> 00:53:55.260
<v Ian Kesler>[BUZZER]
<v Ian Kesler>[蜂鸣器]

00:53:55.260 --> 00:53:55.920
<v Ian Kesler>Katie.
<v Ian Kesler>凯蒂。

00:53:55.920 --> 00:53:57.310
<v Katie>E-I-T-A-K.
<v Katie>E-I-T-A-K。

00:53:57.310 --> 00:53:59.570
<v Ian Kesler>There you go. She's on the board.
<v Ian Kesler>给你。她上板了。

00:53:59.570 --> 00:54:03.800
<v Robin Epstein>Yeah, that wasn't one of my prouder moments as a question writer.
<v Robin Epstein>是啊，那不是我作为出题人最值得骄傲的时刻。

00:54:03.800 --> 00:54:04.740
<v Ian Kesler>Which one would you like to go with?
<v Ian Kesler>你想选哪一个？

00:54:04.740 --> 00:54:05.810
<v Woman 7>I'll take Sign Me Up, please.
<v Woman 7>我选“让我加入”，请。

00:54:05.810 --> 00:54:13.120
<v Ian Kesler>Sign Me Up, all right. This is actually a physical challenge. We want to see who can get an autograph on their arm from the cutest boy in the audience.
<v Ian Kesler>让我加入，好的。这是一个身体挑战。我们想看看谁能让现场最可爱的男孩在她们胳膊上签名。

00:54:13.120 --> 00:54:13.560
<v Ian Kesler>[BUZZER]
<v Ian Kesler>[蜂鸣器]

00:54:13.560 --> 00:54:19.670
<v Ian Kesler>All right, Rachel, here you go. Here's a pen. You've got 15 seconds. Go right now. 15 seconds. Come on, cheer for her. 15 seconds.
<v Ian Kesler>好的，瑞秋，给你。这是笔。你有15秒。现在开始。15秒。来吧，为她欢呼。15秒。

00:54:19.670 --> 00:54:21.130
<v Ian Kesler>[CHEERING]
<v Ian Kesler>[欢呼声]

00:54:21.130 --> 00:54:30.060
<v Ira Glass>So in this one, they're running through the audience with pens. And isn't this pretty much exactly the kind of boy crazy culture that you were trying to not encourage?
<v Ira Glass>所以在这个环节里，她们拿着笔在观众席中奔跑，这不正是你试图不鼓励的那种疯狂追星文化吗？

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:58.210
<v Robin Epstein>Yes. This was pretty much exactly the opposite of what we were trying to show with the show. And that did kind of made me sad that here are these-- they're the ones who are on air and who should be the role models. And it was pretty disappointing to realize that, even though we were trying to find them and to show this, we were failing at it.
<v Robin Epstein>是的。这几乎与我们试图在节目中展示的完全相反。这确实让我有点难过，因为她们是上了电视的人，应该成为榜样。意识到即使我们在努力寻找她们并展示这一点，我们却在失败，这真令人失望。

00:54:58.210 --> 00:54:59.930
<v Ian Kesler>What would be your dream car?
<v Ian Kesler>你的梦幻车型是什么？

00:54:59.930 --> 00:55:02.630
<v Woman 8>Um, I guess my dream car would be a Mustang.
<v Woman 8>嗯，我想我的梦幻车型是一辆野马。

00:55:02.630 --> 00:55:04.060
<v Ian Kesler>A Mustang. Like a new one or an old one?
<v Ian Kesler>一辆野马。新款还是旧款？

00:55:04.060 --> 00:55:05.670
<v Woman 8>Whatever. Like, I probably--
<v Woman 8>随便啦。像，我可能——

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:13.870
<v Ira Glass>And so, when you got into this, your whole idea was, like, OK, let's show how smart girls are. And at the end of this experience, how did you feel?
<v Ira Glass>所以，当你进入这个行业时，你的整个想法是，好，让我们展示一下女孩们有多聪明。经历过这一切后，你感觉如何？

00:55:13.870 --> 00:55:18.000
<v Robin Epstein>I felt girls are dumb.
<v Robin Epstein>我觉得女孩很笨。

00:55:18.000 --> 00:55:19.110
<v Ira Glass>Girls are dumb.
<v Ira Glass>女孩很笨。

00:55:19.110 --> 00:55:20.090
<v Robin Epstein>Girls are dumb.
<v Robin Epstein>女孩很笨。

00:55:20.090 --> 00:55:22.250
<v Ira Glass>Just listen to yourself.
<v Ira Glass>听听你自己说的话。

00:55:22.250 --> 00:55:41.650
<v Robin Epstein>I know. This experience remains one of these things that I'm not entirely sure how to explain. I do think women are smart. But the lesson of what I saw versus what I want to believe is very different.
<v Robin Epstein>我知道。这次经历仍然是我完全不知道该怎么解释的事情之一。我确实认为女性是聪明的。但我所看到的与我想相信的东西之间的教训是非常不同的。

00:55:41.650 --> 00:55:48.390
<v Ira Glass>But is it possible the girls were smart but just not smart in a way that could be made apparent in a trivia quiz?
<v Ira Glass>但有没有可能女孩们很聪明，只是不是那种能通过智力竞赛展现出来的聪明？

00:55:48.390 --> 00:56:14.440
<v Robin Epstein>Yes. I just think that, for a lot of it, the girls seemed much more interested in just sort of showing this really superficial side to themselves, that it was all about sort of what they looked like, and it was all about how they were presenting themselves, as opposed to what was more on the inside, or what seemed like good things to achieve.
<v Robin Epstein>是的。我只是认为，在很大程度上，女孩们似乎对展示自己非常肤浅的一面更感兴趣，即一切都是关于外表，一切都是关于她们如何包装自己，而不是内在有什么，或者什么看起来是值得实现的。

00:56:14.440 --> 00:56:24.600
<v Ira Glass>I wonder if just in the end your mistake was you just thought more people were like you than really are. And that's the mistake that runs the world, basically.
<v Ira Glass>我怀疑最后你的错误只是你认为有更多人像你一样，而不是真的那样。那基本上就是运行世界的错误。

00:56:24.600 --> 00:56:51.340
<v Robin Epstein>Yeah. I mean, I think that's probably the case. Probably not many other girls were spending their weekends playing Trivial Pursuit with their friends. But I no longer feel like it is mine to try and improve the quality of girls. That was maybe an idealistic something that I had in my 20s.
<v Robin Epstein>是啊。我的意思是，我认为情况可能就是那样。可能没多少其他女孩像你一样花周末时间和朋友玩“Trivial Pursuit”智力游戏。但我不再觉得提高女孩素质是我的任务。那可能是我20多岁时的一种理想主义的东西。

00:56:51.340 --> 00:56:55.650
<v Ira Glass>Because you don't think they need it or because you think it's hopeless?
<v Ira Glass>因为你不认为她们需要它，还是因为你认为那没救了？

00:56:55.650 --> 00:56:59.160
<v Robin Epstein>Because I don't-- I'm not sure it's possible.
<v Robin Epstein>因为我不——我不确定那是可能的。

00:56:59.160 --> 00:56:59.830
<v Ira Glass>Yeah?
<v Ira Glass>是吗？

00:56:59.830 --> 00:57:30.920
<v Robin Epstein>Yeah. I'd like not use the word, "hopeless," but hard, certainly. And I don't know what can. I think there's just-- I mean, I think that there can be a role model, per se, that maybe could have this influence. But it's not a game show. Certainly coming up with a quick fix, or a quick answer, or ringing in for something at the buzzer doesn't seem like the long-term solution.
<v Robin Epstein>是的。我不想用“没救了”这个词，但肯定很难。我不知道什么能行。我想那里只是——我的意思是，我认为可以有一个榜样，也许能产生这种影响。但它不是游戏节目。当然，提供快速修复、快速答案或按蜂鸣器回答问题，看起来不像是长期解决方案。

00:57:30.920 --> 00:57:34.630
<v Ira Glass>Robin Epstein in New York.
<v Ira Glass>纽约的罗宾·爱泼斯坦。

00:57:34.630 --> 00:57:45.740
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Alex Blumberg and myself. Bob Harris, the Jeopardy champ that we heard from at the beginning of our program, has written a memoir about how he won and then lost on Jeopardy called Prisoner of Trebekistan.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由亚历克斯·布隆伯格和我自己制作。我们在节目开头听到的《危险边缘》冠军鲍勃·哈里斯写了一本关于他在《危险边缘》获胜又失败的回忆录，书名叫《特雷贝克斯坦的囚徒》。

00:57:45.740 --> 00:58:04.330
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:58:04.330 --> 00:58:14.560
<v Ira Glass>Our website, where you can find our free weekly podcast, absolutely free, www.thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，在那里你可以找到我们的免费每周播客，绝对免费，www.thisamericanlife.org。本节目由国际公共广播电台发行。

00:58:14.560 --> 00:58:29.000
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资助信用]

00:58:29.000 --> 00:58:48.170
<v Ira Glass>And the show that we originally had been planning for this week, with the theme, Tough Room, which includes a visit to the very tough room that is the editorial offices of The Onion , will be here next week. WBEZ management oversight for our program by Mr. Torey Malatia, who has this announcement, that I believe will come as a great relief to mothers everywhere.
<v Ira Glass>我们原定本周播出的主题是“艰难的房间”，其中包括对《洋葱报》编辑室这个非常艰难的房间的访问，下周将播出。本节目的管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚先生负责，他有一个公告，我相信这会让全世界的母亲们感到极大的宽慰。

00:58:48.170 --> 00:58:55.900
<v Robin Epstein>I no longer feel like it is mine to try and improve the quality of girls.
<v Robin Epstein>我不再觉得提高女孩素质是我的任务。

00:58:55.900 --> 00:59:00.280
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。

00:59:00.280 --> 00:59:05.280
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI，国际公共广播电台。
